Okay, I've just added the walking cycle logic to the bots in `moveBot`. That code now increments `b.walkPhase` based on the bot's speed, and also sets `b.moving` to a value between 0 and 1 (capped at a speed of 3 units). I also introduced a new property `_moveT` to give a short “grace period” after a bot stops moving, which will be useful for smoothing out the transition back to an idle pose.

Now I need to make sure that the walking animation actually eases back to idle when the bot stops. Right now, `b.moving` is only set when the bot is actively moving in `moveBot`. If the bot suddenly stops (e.g., reaches its destination or is blocked), `b.moving` will stay at whatever value it had when the bot last moved, because nothing is resetting it. That means the limb animation will remain in a walking pose indefinitely, which is not what I want.

The natural place to handle this decay is in the per‑frame bot update loop where other bot logic runs. Scanning the repository, I see a `grep` result that shows a loop in `app/public/js/game.js`:

```
if (this.state !== 'live') continue;
const head = b.headPos();
const toP = [p.pos[0] - b.pos[0], 0, p.pos[2] - b.pos[2]];
```

This is inside a loop over `this.bots` (probably in a function that processes bot perception or AI). Right after the early‑exit check, I have a perfect hook to update the movement‑related state before any other calculations that depend on `b.moving` or `b._moveT`.

I’ll insert two lines here:
1. Decrease `_moveT` each frame by `dt`, clamping it at zero. This mirrors the logic in `moveBot` where `_moveT` is set to 0.15 when the bot moves; now it will count down when the bot isn’t moving.
2. When `_moveT` reaches zero, start reducing `b.moving` toward zero at a reasonable rate (e.g., `dt * 5`). This will smoothly bring the limb animation back to the idle state.

I need to make sure I reference the `dt` variable that’s already in scope for this loop (the same `dt` used elsewhere for time‑step calculations). I’ll also guard against the case where `_moveT` isn’t defined yet by using `(b._moveT || 0)`.

So the edit will replace the exact snippet:

```
      if (this.state !== 'live') continue;
      const head = b.headPos();
      const toP = [p.pos[0] - b.pos[0], 0, p.pos[2] - b.pos[2]];
```

with the new version that includes the easing logic:

```
      if (this.state !== 'live') continue;
      // ease limbs back to idle when the bot stops
      b._moveT = Math.max(0, (b._moveT || 0) - dt);
      if (b._moveT <= 0) b.moving = Math.max(0, (b.moving || 0) - dt * 5);
      const head = b.headPos();
      const toP = [p.pos[0] - b.pos[0], 0, p.pos[2] - b.pos[2]];
```

This change should integrate cleanly with the existing code, using the same `dt` variable, and will ensure that the walking animation decays naturally when the bot is no longer moving. I'll now issue an Edit tool call with these exact parameters.

